> 
> It should probably be a two-handed weapon. 

Yes, that's a possibility.

> Otherwise it raises the
> question of why you can't use it like a shield of reflection: hold it
> in your offhand and only attack with the primary weapon. Allowing
> that would be overpowered, but not allowing it if it's a one-handed
> weapon seems arbitrary and counter to Nethack's "if it makes sense,
> try it and discover that the DevTeam thought of it first" philosophy.

Well, true, but "arbitrary" is quite a lot in Nethack. Reflection is
such an effect. Amulets are comparably small, so that reflection from
those amulets is probably a magical (no physical) effect, shields and
the silver dragon scales (and for that matter also an amulet are of
reflection) are of convex shape, so should disperse the beam instead
of focussing it at the originating source.

There's another possible explanation that mirrors won't work in the
off-hand; they may require exact positioning to be placed in the beam
(and another level of adjustment to reflect in the direction of its
source because they are plane).

Its effectiveness could be made random to some degree and in addition
dependent of the characters dexterity (say p(refl) = (Dex*6)%, and
another Dex check to determine the direction (0°, 45°, 90°, 135°, left
or right) of the reflection.

Another option is to allow off-hand mirrors but reduce probabilities
to be effective to 10% (absolute, or to 20% of a Dex depending value).

> Worse still, a wizard could wield Magicbane in one hand, a mirror in
> the other, and use thrown daggers and spells to attack. 

The possibility to be able to throw daggers without wielding them and
without unwielding the primary weapon is inherent to Nethack.

